<h2>The facility and the default</h2>
<p>The respondents had taken financial facilities, restructured under a sanction letter of September 2021 totalling about Rs 5.65 crore, secured by a mortgage over property at Tiruppur, Tamil Nadu. The account slipped into NPA classification in September 2026; a loan recall notice followed in October, with the outstanding claimed at Rs 5.29 crore; and the lender issued a demand notice under Section 13(2) of the SARFAESI Act, 2002. Alongside that statutory track, the lender moved the High Court under Section 9 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, seeking ex parte interim measures: disclosure of assets, attachment, freezing of bank accounts, a bank guarantee for the outstanding, and restraint on dealing with the mortgaged property.</p>

<h2>The Court&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Binding guidance in place.</strong> A Division Bench in <em>Axis Finance Ltd. v. Rishab Mago</em> (FAO(OS)(COMM) 157/2026) had upheld dismissal of a Section 9 petition where mortgage security subsisted and no circumstances warranted interim protection.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Distinctions that did not help.</strong> The lender argued it had not taken possession under Section 13(4) and that no Debts Recovery Tribunal proceedings were pending; the Court held the absence of DRT proceedings alone does not create a case for Section 9 relief.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No undertaking offered.</strong> The petitioner declined to undertake not to proceed further under SARFAESI — it sought the statutory remedy and the court&rsquo;s protection at once.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No shown inadequacy.</strong> Nothing on the record demonstrated that the existing mortgage security was insufficient to cover the claim, the basic foundation for supplementary protection.</div>
</div>
<p>Concurrent pursuit of SARFAESI and arbitration remains permissible under <em>M.D. Frozen Foods</em>, but permissibility of parallel remedies is not an entitlement to parallel protections. With a subsisting security interest, an engaged statutory route, and the comfort that any third-party rights created over the mortgaged property would remain subject to the mortgage, the petition was dismissed — expressly without prejudice to other remedies.</p>

<h2>What this means for NBFC and lender practice</h2>
<p>Section 9 petitions have become a standard first strike in lender recoveries, often seeking the full menu of protective orders. This decision, following the Division Bench&rsquo;s lead, marks the limit: the interim jurisdiction protects the eventual award from being rendered illusory, and where the lender already holds registered security adequate to the claim, the award is not in jeopardy. Lenders who genuinely need Section 9 orders should come prepared to show a protection gap — valuations indicating erosion of the security, evidence of dissipation of unsecured assets, or obstacles to statutory enforcement. Borrowers resisting such petitions should put the lender&rsquo;s security package and its SARFAESI steps front and centre, because the overlap is now a recognised ground of refusal.</p>

<h2>The borrower&rsquo;s side of the ledger</h2>
<p>The dismissal is not an escape for borrowers. The mortgage continues to bind the property and anyone who takes it; the SARFAESI timeline continues to run from the Section 13(2) notice, with possession measures under Section 13(4) and the Section 17 remedy before the DRT ahead; and the arbitration itself proceeds to an award that can be enforced as a decree. What the judgment polices is the forum and the sequence, not the debt.</p>
<div class="note">Section 9 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act permits interim measures before or during arbitration and before enforcement of the award; its principles track those governing interim injunctions and attachment before judgment. SARFAESI enforcement is available to banks and notified financial institutions and NBFCs for secured debts. Nothing here is advice in any matter; interim relief always turns on the specific record.</div>
